# CI workflow patterns (GitHub Actions & GitLab CI)

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to workflow-YAML
structure and reuse. Applies only to GitHub Actions workflow files under
`.github/workflows/`, GitHub composite `action.yml`/`action.yaml` files
under `.github/actions/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, and GitLab CI included config
under `.gitlab/` — not to Kubernetes manifests,
Docker Compose files, or any other YAML the `docker-k8s-terraform` pack
already scopes to.

## Trigger and concurrency shape

- Scope `on:`/`rules:` to the events a workflow actually needs (a specific
  branch, path filter, or event type) rather than firing on every push —
  a broad trigger wastes runner minutes and widens the surface a
  misconfigured job can affect.
- Add a `concurrency:` group (GitHub Actions) or a `resource_group` (GitLab
  CI) to any workflow that deploys or mutates shared state, so a second
  push does not race an in-flight run against the same target — a
  `resource_group` serializes/queues jobs targeting the same resource,
  which is the property a deploy job needs. Do not reach for
  `interruptible: true` here: that flag means the OPPOSITE (this job is
  SAFE to auto-cancel when a newer pipeline starts), which is right for a
  build/test job made obsolete by a newer commit, not for a deploy already
  in flight.
- Keep `pull_request` as the default trigger for anything that only builds
  or tests untrusted contributions; reach for `pull_request_target` only
  when the job genuinely needs base-repo secrets, and see
  `rules/security.mdc` for what that requires.

## Job structure and reuse

- Give every job an explicit `name:` and a `timeout-minutes` (GitHub
  Actions) or job-level `timeout` (GitLab CI) — an unbounded job can hang
  a runner indefinitely on a stuck process or a flaky network call.
- Factor a step sequence used by more than one workflow into a reusable
  workflow (`workflow_call`) or composite action (GitHub Actions), or an
  `include:`/`extends:` template (GitLab CI), instead of copy-pasting the
  same steps into every workflow file — the copy drifts the moment one
  copy gets a fix the others do not.
- Pin a `runs-on`/`image:` to a specific, known runner or image tag rather
  than a rolling `latest`, so a runner image update cannot silently change
  build behavior underneath an unrelated change.
- Use a job-level `needs:` (GitHub Actions) or `needs:`/stage ordering
  (GitLab CI) to express real dependencies between jobs explicitly, rather
  than relying on default stage ordering to imply an ordering the config
  does not actually state.

## Caching and artifacts

- Key a cache (`actions/cache`, GitLab's `cache:key`) off the lockfile or
  dependency manifest hash, not a static string — a static key serves a
  stale cache forever and a hash-based key invalidates exactly when the
  dependency set actually changes.
- Scope `artifacts:`/`actions/upload-artifact` to the files a later job
  actually consumes, with an explicit retention/expiry, rather than
  uploading an entire working directory that balloons storage and
  transfer time.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A workflow that duplicates the same 10+ line step sequence across
  multiple jobs or files instead of a reusable workflow, composite
  action, or `include:`/`extends:` template.
- A job with no `timeout-minutes`/`timeout` on a step that can hang (a
  network call, a wait loop, an interactive prompt the runner cannot
  answer).
- A cache key that never changes (a literal string with no hash of the
  lockfile), which either never invalidates or never actually caches
  anything useful.
